Suppose you applied to 100 jobs last month and received only 2 interview calls. The instinctive solution is: "Next month I'll apply to 300." But that only works if application volume was the problem.

Imagine instead that recruiters are rejecting your profile because your resume doesn't communicate your experience at the level you're targeting. Or you're applying to roles where your experience doesn't match the hiring team's requirement. Or you're relying entirely on job portals while candidates entering through referrals and recruiter outreach are getting reviewed first.

Then increasing application volume doesn't fix anything. It simply increases the number of times the same broken process gets repeated.

That's why the first question shouldn't be "How many jobs should I apply to?" It should be "Where exactly is my interview pipeline breaking?"

